  "scenario": "MPLS L3VPN VPNv4 Route Reflector",
  "goals": "• {{router1_name}}, {{router2_name}} and {{router3_name}} are provider edge routers in AS {{as_number}}, each connected only to {{router4_name}} in the middle. OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured and operational on all three links.\n• Every PE already holds the VPN routing instance {{vrf_name}} with route target {{rt}} and a customer Loopback1 inside it:\n    {{router1_name}}: route distinguisher {{rd1}}, {{router1_loopback1_cidr}}\n    {{router2_name}}: route distinguisher {{rd2}}, {{router2_loopback1_cidr}}\n    {{router3_name}}: route distinguisher {{rd3}}, {{router3_loopback1_cidr}}\n• No BGP is configured yet. Do not build a full mesh of VPNv4 sessions between the three PEs: each PE may have exactly one BGP session, towards {{router4_name}} ({{router4_loopback0_ip}}), sourced from Loopback0.\n• Make {{router4_name}} reflect VPNv4 routes between the three PEs by treating all three of them as route reflector clients, and make sure extended communities are exchanged on every session.\n• {{router4_name}} has no VPN routing instance of its own, so by default it would discard the VPNv4 routes whose route targets it does not import. Keep those routes on {{router4_name}} so that reflection actually works.\n• Every PE must also announce its own customer Loopback1 network into the VPN address family of {{vrf_name}} by redistributing its connected routes there, otherwise there is nothing for {{router4_name}} to reflect.\n• Result: every PE must learn the two remote customer prefixes with the remote PE Loopback0 as next hop, and the customer loopbacks must be able to ping each other inside {{vrf_name}}.",
